import{_ as e,o as s,c as a,ag as n}from"./chunks/framework.2cFhd8Ux.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Local Models: Ollama on a Laptop and on the Lab Machine","description":"","frontmatter":{"source":"setup/local-models.md"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"setup/local-models.md","filePath":"setup/local-models.md"}'),o={name:"setup/local-models.md"};function i(l,t,h,r,p,d){return s(),a("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[n(`<h1 id="local-models-ollama-on-a-laptop-and-on-the-lab-machine" tabindex="-1">Local Models: Ollama on a Laptop and on the Lab Machine <a class="header-anchor" href="#local-models-ollama-on-a-laptop-and-on-the-lab-machine" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Local Models: Ollama on a Laptop and on the Lab Machine&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Unlimited, private inference for the kit labs and evals, if you configure the context window. Model names, sizes and versions are in <a href="./../TOOLS.html#the-lab-machine-and-local-models">Tool Handout: The lab machine and local models</a>.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why the course uses local models.</strong> Free API tiers are small and may train on your prompts. A local model costs nothing per request and keeps prompts on the machine. It is also weaker than a frontier model, and that is useful: Michigan&#39;s EECS 498 runs on small local models on purpose, because &quot;a small model punishes sloppy engineering visibly&quot; (<a href="https://eecs498-aase.github.io/syllabus.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Michigan syllabus</a>). Use local models for <code>mini-agent</code>, <code>mcp-from-scratch</code>, <code>eval-harness</code> (as subject or judge) and small fixes. Don&#39;t expect them to navigate the class codebase the way a frontier model does.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>What it runs</th><th>Who it&#39;s for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>The lab machine</strong> (a 24–32 GB GPU)</td><td>Mid-size coding models at a 64K context, served to the class as one queue</td><td>Everyone, through its API</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Your laptop</strong> (16 GB of RAM or more)</td><td>Small models, for the kit labs</td><td>Optional; a private, always-available fallback</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Neither</strong></td><td>—</td><td>The free API tiers cover every lab (<a href="./api-keys.html#switching-provider-or-using-the-lab-machine">API Keys, Tokens and Spending Caps: Switching provider, or using the lab machine</a>)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="the-64k-context-setting-and-why-tool-calls-fail-silently-without-it" tabindex="-1">The 64K-context setting, and why tool calls fail silently without it <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-64k-context-setting-and-why-tool-calls-fail-silently-without-it" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The 64K-context setting, and why tool calls fail silently without it&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A model&#39;s <strong>context window</strong> is how many tokens it can read in one request: system prompt, tool schemas, conversation and file contents together. Ollama sets a default context from your GPU memory, and on most laptops and smaller GPUs the default is a few thousand tokens (TOOLS.md has the thresholds). An agent&#39;s first request is larger than that: the system prompt plus the JSON schema of every tool is often several thousand tokens before you&#39;ve asked anything.</p><p>When a request is longer than the context, Ollama <strong>truncates it and carries on</strong>. There is no error. The model simply never sees part of the prompt, typically the instructions and tool definitions. What you see:</p><ul><li>the model answers in prose, or prints a bash command as text, instead of returning a <code>tool_calls</code> entry;</li><li>the agent loop &quot;finishes&quot; after one turn because the reply has no tool call;</li><li>behavior changes when the conversation gets longer, though nothing in your code changed.</li></ul><p>Ollama&#39;s documentation says agents and coding tools should be given <strong>at least 64,000 tokens</strong> (<a href="https://docs.ollama.com/context-length" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Ollama: context length</a>). The setting is per server, through an environment variable, because the OpenAI-compatible endpoint the kits use has no per-request context option:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">64000</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ollama</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> serve</span></span></code></pre></div><p>A larger context needs more memory. If the model plus a 64K context doesn&#39;t fit in GPU memory, Ollama moves part of it to the CPU and everything slows down sharply; that&#39;s the signal to use a smaller model, not a smaller context.</p><h2 id="on-a-laptop" tabindex="-1">On a laptop <a class="header-anchor" href="#on-a-laptop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;On a laptop&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><p><strong>Check your memory.</strong> 16 GB of RAM is the practical minimum for the small models TOOLS.md lists. Close other heavy programs while a model runs.</p></li><li><p><strong>Install Ollama</strong> from <a href="https://ollama.com/download" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ollama.com/download</a>. It installs a local server on port 11434.</p></li><li><p><strong>Start the server with the 64K context.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Linux, or any system from a terminal:</strong> quit any running Ollama, then run <code>OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH=64000 ollama serve</code> and leave that terminal open.</li><li><strong>macOS app:</strong> <code>launchctl setenv OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH 64000</code>, then quit and reopen the Ollama app.</li><li><strong>Windows app:</strong> add a user environment variable <code>OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH</code> = <code>64000</code> (Settings → System → About → Advanced system settings → Environment Variables), then quit and restart Ollama.</li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Pull the laptop model</strong> named in TOOLS.md:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">ollama</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> pull</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> [laptop </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">model</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> from</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> TOOLS.md]</span></span></code></pre></div></li><li><p><strong>Point a kit at it</strong>, in the kit&#39;s <code>.env</code>:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>LLM_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>LLM_API_KEY=ollama</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>LLM_MODEL=[laptop model from TOOLS.md]</span></span></code></pre></div></li><li><p><strong>Run the smoke test below.</strong></p></li></ol><p>A Codespace can&#39;t reach <code>localhost</code> on your laptop. To use a laptop model, run the kit on your laptop, ideally in a dev container (<a href="./sandboxes.html#dev-containers-on-your-laptop">Sandboxes: Codespaces and Dev Containers: Dev containers on your laptop</a>).</p><h2 id="the-tool-calling-smoke-test" tabindex="-1">The tool-calling smoke test <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-tool-calling-smoke-test" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The tool-calling smoke test&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Run it once with no padding, then with padding that pushes the request past a small default context. Both should return a tool call. If the padded one doesn&#39;t, the context setting isn&#39;t in effect.</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">export</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> LLM_BASE_URL</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">http://localhost:11434/v1</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">export</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> LLM_MODEL</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;[model from TOOLS.md]&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">export</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> PAD</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">0</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">      # then try PAD=2000</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">python3</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> -</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> &lt;&lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&#39;EOF&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> curl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -s</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;\${</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">LLM_BASE_URL</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">%/</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">}/chat/completions&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -H</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;Content-Type: application/json&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -H</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;Authorization: Bearer \${</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">LLM_API_KEY</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">:-</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">ollama</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">}&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --data</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> @-</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> python3</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -c</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;import json,sys; m=json.load(sys.stdin)[&#39;choices&#39;][0][&#39;message&#39;]; print(&#39;TOOL CALL:&#39;, m[&#39;tool_calls&#39;][0][&#39;function&#39;]) if m.get(&#39;tool_calls&#39;) else print(&#39;NO TOOL CALL:&#39;, (m.get(&#39;content&#39;) or &#39;&#39;)[:200])&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">import json, os</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">pad = &quot;This sentence is padding for the context-length check. &quot; * int(os.environ.get(&quot;PAD&quot;, &quot;0&quot;))</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">print(json.dumps({</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">    &quot;model&quot;: os.environ[&quot;LLM_MODEL&quot;],</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">    &quot;messages&quot;: [</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">        {&quot;role&quot;: &quot;system&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: &quot;You are a coding agent. Always use a tool to inspect files. &quot; + pad},</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">        {&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: &quot;Which files are in the folder src? Use your tool.&quot;},</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">    ],</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">    &quot;tools&quot;: [{&quot;type&quot;: &quot;function&quot;, &quot;function&quot;: {</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">        &quot;name&quot;: &quot;list_files&quot;,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">        &quot;description&quot;: &quot;List the files in a folder of the workspace&quot;,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">        &quot;parameters&quot;: {&quot;type&quot;: &quot;object&quot;, &quot;properties&quot;: {&quot;path&quot;: {&quot;type&quot;: &quot;string&quot;}}, &quot;required&quot;: [&quot;path&quot;]},</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">    }}],</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">}))</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">EOF</span></span></code></pre></div><p>✅ <strong>Checkpoint:</strong> both runs print <code>TOOL CALL:</code> with <code>list_files</code> and a path like <code>src</code>. Record the model, the padding and the result in your <code>AI_LOG.md</code>: that&#39;s an <strong>observed</strong> claim about your setup. The same script works against the lab machine or a free API: change <code>LLM_BASE_URL</code>, <code>LLM_MODEL</code> and <code>LLM_API_KEY</code>.</p><p>If the unpadded run already prints <code>NO TOOL CALL</code>, the model itself isn&#39;t reliable at tool calling, whatever the context. Some small models print the command they would run instead of calling the tool. Try the next model in TOOLS.md.</p><h2 id="on-the-lab-machine" tabindex="-1">On the lab machine <a class="header-anchor" href="#on-the-lab-machine" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;On the lab machine&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The instructor runs Ollama on a lab machine with a 24–32 GB GPU, at a 64K context, with the models TOOLS.md lists. You use its API; you never log in to it.</p><ul><li><strong>Connecting.</strong> Your instructor gives you the host name and says how to reach it: from the campus network or VPN, or through an authenticated endpoint. A Codespace runs in the cloud, outside the campus network, so it can reach the lab machine only through that endpoint. Set <code>LLM_BASE_URL=http://[lab-machine-host]:11434/v1</code> (or the address you&#39;re given) and <code>LLM_MODEL</code> to the lab model.</li><li><strong>It&#39;s one queue for the whole class.</strong> One GPU serves one or a few requests at a time. A request may wait behind a team&#39;s batch. Expect seconds to minutes per agent turn at busy times.</li><li><strong>Be a good neighbor.</strong> Batch work by team rather than running 30 agent loops at once; keep contexts lean; set a step limit and a timeout; don&#39;t leave a loop running unattended; retry with backoff on errors.</li><li><strong>The data rule still applies.</strong> Prompts stay on the machine, which makes it the safest place to send a stack trace from your own code. It is still shared course infrastructure: no personal data, no secrets (<a href="./safety-contract.html#1-the-data-rule">contract rule 1</a>).</li><li><strong>When it&#39;s down or saturated,</strong> switch to a free API by configuration (<a href="./api-keys.html#switching-provider-or-using-the-lab-machine">API Keys, Tokens and Spending Caps: Switching provider, or using the lab machine</a>). That&#39;s the planned fallback, not an emergency.</li></ul><h2 id="free-agent-harnesses-on-a-local-model" tabindex="-1">Free agent harnesses on a local model <a class="header-anchor" href="#free-agent-harnesses-on-a-local-model" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Free agent harnesses on a local model&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Several CLI agents run against a local Ollama at no cost; TOOLS.md lists which, with the commands. Two cautions:</p><ul><li><strong>Each harness sends a much larger system prompt than <code>mini-agent</code> does.</strong> A harness that works on a 64K context may fail on a smaller one in exactly the silent way described above. OpenCode&#39;s own documentation requires 64K or more.</li><li><strong>Some harnesses route requests to a hosted service by default.</strong> Check where requests go before you assume a session is local, and keep keys in your own environment variables rather than a hosted console.</li></ul><h2 id="troubleshooting" tabindex="-1">Troubleshooting <a class="header-anchor" href="#troubleshooting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Troubleshooting&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The model replies in prose or prints a command instead of calling the tool → <a href="./../resources/troubleshooting.html#the-local-model-ignores-its-tools">the local model ignores its tools</a></li><li>Everything is very slow → the model plus context doesn&#39;t fit in GPU memory; use the smaller model TOOLS.md suggests</li><li><code>connection refused</code> on port 11434 → the server isn&#39;t running, or (for the lab machine) you&#39;re off the campus network: <a href="./../resources/troubleshooting.html#the-lab-machine-is-slow-or-unreachable">the lab machine is slow or unreachable</a></li></ul><p>Back to <a href="./">Week 0</a></p>`,28)])])}const k=e(o,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,k as default};
